VIGIL in one line. A web app an astronaut opens every day to check their own health. It compares each number with their own pre-flight baseline and with published medical and NASA limits, then shows a colour-coded answer with the reason, the source, and what to do.

The problem it solves. On a Mars transit, a message to the flight surgeon on Earth takes 4 to 24 minutes one way, so a round trip can be 48 minutes. The crew has to assess first and the ground confirms later. NASA's Human Research Program lists five hazards of spaceflight: radiation, isolation and confinement, distance from Earth, altered gravity, and a hostile closed environment. Each one shows up first as a small shift in an ordinary number, like a shorter night, a slower reaction time, or a few extra beats per minute at rest. VIGIL watches those shifts for the crew.

What it tracks. Seventeen indicators (systolic and diastolic blood pressure count separately). Six come straight from the challenge brief: blood pressure, heart rate, blood glucose, body temperature, sleep, and mood. We added blood oxygen, breathing rate, heart rate variability, body mass, exercise minutes, stress, sleepiness (Karolinska scale), reaction-test speed and lapses, and daily radiation dose, plus a symptom checklist with urgent items marked in red.

How data gets in. Typed on a keyboard or tapped on a touch screen, pre-filled from a wearable, imported from a CSV file, read by the phone camera (20 seconds of fingertip or face video turned into a heart rate on the device), or produced by the built-in 60-second reaction test, which is a short form of the test ISS crews already run.

How it decides. Two comparisons run on every value. First, published ranges: blood pressure categories from the American Heart Association, fever from the CDC, glucose from the American Diabetes Association, radiation limits from NASA's own standard. Second, the person's own baseline: fourteen pre-flight days give a median and a typical spread per indicator, so a resting heart rate of 95 is flagged for someone whose normal is 55 even though 95 is inside the adult range. Three-day trends count too.

What the crew sees. Three levels: Nominal, Watch, Act now. Every alert has four parts: why it fired with the actual numbers, what to do, the source it comes from, and a checklist. If the ground needs to know, the app drafts one message with the whole day's values and flags so a single exchange across the delay is enough.

The screens. Today screen with the day's status, the single next action and one tile per indicator. Daily check-in. Reaction test. Trend charts with the baseline band and the published cut-offs drawn on them. A body map that colours seven body systems from the same rule results and shows which hazards affect each one. Alerts with checklists and the surgeon message, plus a crew-to-ground link that delivers the report after the real light-time delay (with a demo mode that plays 1 minute as 1 second) when the ground console is open in a second window. A daily routine with timed prompts, a streak, and mission patches for habits such as steady daily measurement. A ground view showing what the flight surgeon receives. A learn section explaining every indicator and every source. Settings for the environment (station, transit, rover, base), the demo scenario, and the mission day.

The demo. The crew is simulated and labelled as such everywhere. Four scenarios make the rules visibly fire: a quiet nominal mission, a solar storm that turns the whole crew red, a week of sleep debt that slows the Commander's reactions, and an early infection that flags Flight Engineer 1 with a fever.

What makes it credible. Every threshold is cited inside the alert and listed in the app. No real astronaut records are used. It runs offline, installs like an app on a phone or tablet, keeps data on the device, and has over 100 automated tests covering every rule at its cited cut-off.

What it is not. Not a medical device. It never diagnoses. It supports the crew's decision and points to the flight surgeon when a rule says so.

Where it lives. Live at https://victor-andiazabal.github.io/victor-andiazabal/. Code, deck, video script, submission draft and pitch notes are in the `vigil` folder of your GitHub repo. To run it on your own computer, see the Try it section of vigil/README.md (double-click start-windows.bat or start-mac.command).
